WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.230
PBS Kids opens worlds of possibilities for all children

00:00:03.230 --> 00:00:05.630
thanks to PBS stations and viewers like you.

00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:20.940
Twice as high, it's Santa Fe.

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:44.740
Where it's in

00:01:04.990 --> 00:01:09.030
Hi. Look around. What do you see?

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:12.980
This is a very special time that happens every day

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:17.140
when the colors deepen and the shadows lengthen

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:20.140
and the earth speaks softly of her beauty.

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:22.130
It's almost sunset.

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:26.350
Dominic Arcaro is a Native American artist

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:29.980
who is trying to capture the beauty he sees in this sky.

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:38.390
I love sunsets because of the colors, because they're never the same.

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:51.130
I look into my memory of all the sunsets I've seen, and I remember the colors,

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:58.860
white to a yellow to an orange to a red spectrum, and into the darker blues and violets.

00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:06.480
My name is Dominic Arcaro. I'm from Cochiti Pueblo, New Mexico.

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:14.760
My Indian name is Kishiwa. It means runner. It was given to me by my grandfather.

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:27.260
Painting to Native Americans has held a very special place in our lives since the beginning of our time.

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:29.980
We painted out of a...

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:40.400
Just as we sang, danced, and prayed, I still paint out of a need, a need to create to feel colors and motion.

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:48.880
All paints come from Mother Earth, all paints, all colors.

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:59.500
Black is usually taken from a burning process of certain weeds.

00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:01.960
and adding water to that.

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:10.700
Red can be obtained directly from the ground

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:13.480
through clays or mineral veins.

00:03:17.210 --> 00:03:20.810
Yellow can be obtained from different types of minerals

00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:22.870
like iron oxide.

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:37.950
When the sun sets, it gives birth to a new variety of animals.

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:40.970
They come out and feed and graze throughout the night.

00:03:50.570 --> 00:03:53.270
I say, go where your heart is needed.

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:57.480
Let your inside self be the master of the brush.

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:16.020
did you ever see something so beautiful that you wanted to hold on to it forever well a boy named

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:23.100
little gopher did and at first he didn't know how but he listened very carefully to the earth

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:29.760
and finally she showed him the way in this story the legend of the indian paintbrush

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:46.680
The Legend of the Indian Paintbrush, retold and illustrated by Tommy DePaola, read by Harold Littlebird.

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:55.680
Many years ago, when the people traveled the plains and lived in a circle of tipis,

00:04:56.430 --> 00:04:59.980
there was a boy who was smaller than the rest of the children in the country.

00:05:01.210 --> 00:05:10.450
No matter how hard he tried, he couldn't keep up with the other boys who were always riding and shooting their bows to prove their strength.

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:16.620
But the boy, who was called Little Gopher, was not without a gift of his own.

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:22.340
From an early age, he loved to decorate stones with the juices from berries.

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:28.670
The wise shaman of the tribe understood that Little Gopher had a gift that was special.

00:05:30.190 --> 00:05:35.060
Do not struggle, little gopher. Your path will not be the same as the others.

00:05:36.260 --> 00:05:43.500
They will grow up to be warriors. Your place among the people will be remembered for a different reason.

00:05:46.970 --> 00:05:52.250
In a few years, little gopher went out to the hills alone to think about becoming a man,

00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:59.310
for this was the custom of the tribe, and it was there that a dream vision came to him.

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:07.390
The sky filled with clouds, and out of them came a young Indian maiden and an old grandfather.

00:06:07.390 --> 00:06:16.180
She carried a rolled-up animal skin, and he carried pots of paint and a brush made of fine animal hairs.

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:18.860
The grandfather spoke,

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:26.050
My son, these are the tools by which you shall become great among your people.

00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:29.730
You will paint pictures of the deeds of the warriors.

00:06:30.350 --> 00:06:35.420
and the visions of the shaman, and the people shall see them and remember them forever.

00:06:37.390 --> 00:06:42.260
The maiden unrolled a pure white buckskin and placed it on the ground.

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:46.960
Find a buckskin as white as this, she told him.

00:06:48.330 --> 00:06:55.090
Keep it, and one day you will paint a picture that is as pure as the colors in the evening sky.

00:06:57.260 --> 00:06:59.260
And as she finished speaking,

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:04.430
The clouds cleared, and a sunset of great beauty filled the sky.

00:07:06.490 --> 00:07:11.830
Little Gopher looked at the white buckskin, and on it he saw colors as bright and beautiful

00:07:11.830 --> 00:07:14.300
as those made by the setting sun.

00:07:17.590 --> 00:07:22.620
Then the sun sank behind the hills, and the dream vision was over.

00:07:24.980 --> 00:07:29.980
The next day, Little Gopher began to make brushes from the hairs of...

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:38.280
different animals. He gathered berries and flowers and rocks of different colors and crushed them to

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:53.640
make his paints. And he began to paint pictures of great hunts, of great deeds, of great dream

00:07:53.640 --> 00:07:57.900
visions, so that the people would always remember.

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:04.430
Many months before, he had found his pure white buckskin,

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:09.930
but it remained empty because he could not find the colors of the sunset.

00:08:10.810 --> 00:08:17.060
He used the brightest flowers, the reddest berries, and the deepest purples from the rocks,

00:08:17.660 --> 00:08:20.740
and still his paintings never satisfied him.

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:27.690
He began to go to the top of a hill each evening and look at the colors that filled the sky

00:08:27.690 --> 00:08:29.650
to try and understand...

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:40.950
how to make them one night he heard a voice calling to him because you have been faithful

00:08:40.950 --> 00:08:49.730
to the people and true to your gift you shall find the colors you are seeking the next evening

00:08:49.850 --> 00:08:56.130
little gopher went to the top of the hill as the colors of the sunset spread across the sky and

00:08:57.560 --> 00:08:59.970
There, on the ground all around him.

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:04.990
were brushes filled with paint, each one a color of the sunset.

00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:14.360
Little Gopher began to paint quickly and surely, using one brush, then another.

00:09:16.210 --> 00:09:21.890
And as the colors in the sky began to fade, Little Gopher gazed at the white buckskin.

00:09:22.330 --> 00:09:27.160
And he was happy. He had found the colors of the sunset.

00:09:29.260 --> 00:09:29.980
He carried his...

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:38.000
painting down to the circle of the people leaving the brushes on the hillside and the next day when

00:09:38.500 --> 00:09:45.550
the people awoke the hill was ablaze with color for the brushes had taken root in the earth and

00:09:45.550 --> 00:09:54.200
multiplied into plants of brilliant reds oranges and yellows and every spring from that time the

00:09:54.250 --> 00:09:59.890
hills and meadows burst into bloom and the people no longer

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:07.320
called him little gopher, but he who brought the sunset to the earth.

00:10:28.850 --> 00:10:29.470
It's morning.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:33.420
and the mountains of New Mexico are sparkling with snow.

00:10:35.380 --> 00:10:37.360
Here is the Taos Pueblo,

00:10:37.820 --> 00:10:41.360
home to the Taos people for over a thousand years.

00:10:42.390 --> 00:10:45.250
They call themselves the Red Willow People,

00:10:45.290 --> 00:10:48.270
and they call the earth their mother.

00:10:50.170 --> 00:10:54.050
Every part of their lives is in some way connected to the land.

00:10:55.140 --> 00:10:57.640
Even their homes are made from the ground that they walk on.

00:10:57.800 --> 00:10:59.530
It's called adobe.

00:11:00.090 --> 00:11:03.320
and the bricks are made from a mixture of mud, water, and straw,

00:11:03.420 --> 00:11:04.380
baked hard in the sun.

00:11:06.120 --> 00:11:10.370
People believe that all life comes from the earth.

00:11:12.280 --> 00:11:13.440
It's where their art begins, too.

00:11:16.880 --> 00:11:22.020
This red clay will be shaped into some of the most beautiful pottery in the world.

00:11:22.700 --> 00:11:24.240
Okay, maybe we should put some of the...

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:29.730
The Naranjo family, Dolly, her mother, Rose, and her daughter, Jody.

00:11:30.510 --> 00:11:33.850
make pots from this earth on the Santa Clara Pueblo

00:11:33.850 --> 00:11:36.040
as their grandmothers did before them.

00:11:39.020 --> 00:11:42.520
The mixing of the clay is one of the special parts

00:11:42.520 --> 00:11:43.840
of making pottery.

00:11:45.780 --> 00:11:46.940
Oh, it's cold.

00:11:47.840 --> 00:11:50.500
The two types of clay are mixed together

00:11:50.500 --> 00:11:53.940
to give the red clay substance.

00:11:54.960 --> 00:11:56.280
I am Dolly Naranjo.

00:11:56.580 --> 00:11:59.800
My family has been making pottery for...

00:12:00.220 --> 00:12:12.640
generations the first moment of stepping into it how cold it is and after a few

00:12:12.640 --> 00:12:19.600
moments you know it warms up it your your body responds to it this matter

00:12:19.600 --> 00:12:25.780
that's extremely silky and soft it becomes a part of you

00:12:48.110 --> 00:12:55.450
not quite mixed yet because they're white flecks in there that you can see and if you don't get the

00:12:55.450 --> 00:12:59.920
white flecks mixed in then when you're making the pot

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:19.670
It leaves holes when you've got your feet or your arms and clay. It truly does feel like you're in chocolate pudding

00:13:31.760 --> 00:13:43.350
I learned how to make pots in the same way that every child who makes pottery in the Pueblos learns to make pottery, which is by watching and doing.

00:13:50.120 --> 00:13:59.530
The clay is so, so special, and you never waste it. You never, never throw any of it away. You never...

00:14:00.320 --> 00:14:16.430
are disrespectful to the clay in any way pottery allows me to feel that i am a part of the world

00:14:16.430 --> 00:14:24.590
that we live in and it allows me to be creative and it allows me to express myself

00:14:36.560 --> 00:14:46.240
when you begin working and making a pot it forms itself and when the pot is ready to close then

00:14:46.240 --> 00:14:54.760
it comes together and when it's finished it's finished but it's impossible to try and make a

00:14:54.760 --> 00:14:56.680
pot be something that it doesn't want to be

00:15:01.690 --> 00:15:02.450
And it's done.

00:15:30.120 --> 00:15:32.500
I have many, many, many polishing stones.

00:15:33.410 --> 00:15:41.070
The stones that I have were given to me by my mother when I first started making pottery.

00:15:56.980 --> 00:15:59.960
The firing is to harden the pot.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:02.930
It makes the pots permanent.

00:16:08.000 --> 00:16:09.570
The tin is right there.

00:16:31.820 --> 00:16:34.840
Pottery making is just simply a part of your daily life.

00:16:35.000 --> 00:16:38.610
It's what we do.

00:16:39.030 --> 00:16:40.410
It's who we are.

00:16:41.210 --> 00:16:45.080
It is our connection to the earth.

00:17:18.600 --> 00:17:29.980
First winter snow, a magical time.

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:37.130
for the pueblo people they are grateful for this snow because it will melt and feed this creek

00:17:38.760 --> 00:17:59.850
listen to the red willow creek its music and movement provide inspiration to the taos red willow dance

00:18:01.020 --> 00:18:07.440
Benito Concha and his family use all the gifts of the earth to create a living art, their dance.

00:18:13.240 --> 00:18:22.100
My family and I dance, so then the songs and the dances will be passed on to generations and generations.

00:18:23.130 --> 00:18:28.600
They will also feel the beauty and the love and the sacredness in all the dances.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:32.910
My name is Benito Concha, I'm from Taos Pueblo.

00:18:33.970 --> 00:18:35.770
My people are the Red Willow people.

00:18:35.950 --> 00:18:39.380
We live in the mountains of northern New Mexico.

00:18:39.620 --> 00:18:46.220
When I was first coming into dancing as a child,

00:18:47.340 --> 00:18:49.740
I remember my father dressing me,

00:18:50.930 --> 00:18:54.740
and he told me the purpose for each part of that outfit.

00:18:54.840 --> 00:18:58.680
When my father dresses up my nephews,

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:02.330
He's doing the same thing he did for me.

00:19:10.260 --> 00:19:16.160
Each person's outfit is expressing their feelings

00:19:16.160 --> 00:19:17.300
that come from within them.

00:19:20.560 --> 00:19:27.470
The shawl, their moccasins, their leggings, their dress.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:35.370
The bright eyes and the feathers, they wear their hair, they all have a purpose, and all

00:19:35.650 --> 00:19:38.910
of it comes from the earth, and it is representing the earth.

00:19:42.770 --> 00:19:47.390
Whenever there are ceremonies, we wear this face paint.

00:19:48.260 --> 00:19:56.840
It is a red paint that my people have always acquired through the land, and the red represents

00:19:56.840 --> 00:19:57.700
the earth itself.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:02.180
The white represents the people on the air.

00:20:09.440 --> 00:20:12.430
I learned the eagle dance at a very early age.

00:20:13.450 --> 00:20:18.010
All Native Americans feel significance of the eagle

00:20:18.740 --> 00:20:24.240
as a highly honored being, for it carries our prayers.

00:20:24.440 --> 00:20:26.320
And it's the being that can fly the highest.

00:20:28.380 --> 00:20:29.820
I see the movement of the eagle.

00:20:30.440 --> 00:20:31.340
and that is my teacher.

00:20:33.840 --> 00:20:36.680
When I dance, I feel like I'm an eagle,

00:20:37.870 --> 00:20:40.780
and every time I put on the eagle wings to dance,

00:20:41.690 --> 00:20:43.790
I do it in honor and respect for the eagle.

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:06.320
I love you, I love you, I love you, I love you

00:22:15.930 --> 00:22:20.020
The story of Little Gopher and his vision quest is a legend of the Plains Indian.

00:22:20.900 --> 00:22:25.510
There are many different Native American tribes, each with its own legends and history.

00:22:26.300 --> 00:22:27.840
Here are some of my favorites.

00:22:31.370 --> 00:22:40.070
Once upon a time, long before humans were in this world, crows were beautiful and as colourful as a rainbow.

00:22:41.290 --> 00:22:46.340
This legend tells their story. It's called Rainbow Crow.

00:22:48.610 --> 00:22:53.030
In those days, the weather was warm and the animals were happy.

00:22:54.350 --> 00:22:59.530
Then, snow began to fall. It became deeper.

00:23:00.090 --> 00:23:09.780
and burl. Soon the animals were covered with snow. Animals decided the only way to save

00:23:09.780 --> 00:23:20.330
the day was to send a messenger to the great spirit. Crow was chosen. The great spirit liked

00:23:20.330 --> 00:23:29.980
Crow and gave him the gift of fire. I'm Baba Moy and I really loved the magical legend.

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:39.940
of the Rainbow Crow. I think you will too. Hi, my name is Jamon Martinez. Do you think you know a lot

